// docs/claude/work/PIPELINE.jsonl — THE FOLLOW-THROUGH PIPELINE. Append-only.
// Schema, the five failure reasons it answers, and every invariant:
//   scripts/ops/pipeline.py  (python3 scripts/ops/pipeline.py --self-test)
// Design of record: docs/plans/OPERATING-PLAN-2026-09-21.md § 3b.
//
// APPEND A LINE. NEVER EDIT ONE. A state change is a NEW record; the current
// state of an item is its LAST record, and the log is the audit trail. This is
// JSONL and not a JSON array precisely because a shared array is what produced
// the archived registers' merge conflicts.
//
// An item may NOT leave without a terminal_reason. `killed` is a first-class,
// respectable outcome — most of the 1,065 archived backlog rows should be
// killed explicitly, and recording that is worth more than carrying them.
//
// ⚠️ SEEDED EMPTY ON PURPOSE. The 91 OPEN-ITEMS monitoring rows (each already
// carrying a `clears_when`) and the 1,065 archived backlog rows are A8's
// import, not A7's. Importing them unvalidated would fill the operator's
// section 0 with rows nobody triaged, which is the failure this replaces.
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Either the trainer VM has a serialization mechanism that actually exists, or those comments say plainly that heavy work is unserialized and what the consequence is. Reading the comments is not enough -- check whether two concurrent trainer jobs can in fact overlap today.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0001", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP, found via scope-overlap-audit's self-test failing on PR #12674 for a missing board-pointer.json", "rerun": "grep -rln 'board-pointer\\|board_pointer' --include=*.yml --include=*.py . | grep -v '^./docs/archive'"}, "state": "queued", "what": "The trainer VM's cross-session serialization protocol is gone: trainer-diag-relay.yml and trainer-vm-diag.yml both document heavy VM work as serialized by a VM-LANE CLAIM/RELEASE FIFO on the coordination board, which the 2026-09-21 reset archived. Comments only -- the relays still RUN -- but on a 1-OCPU box the serialization they promise no longer exists and nothing says so."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Every one of the 10 either no longer references the archived path, or carries a dated note saying why it stays. The COUNT going to zero is the check.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0002", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "E9 / PR #12674", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import pathlib;print([p for p in pathlib.Path('.').rglob('*') if p.suffix in {'.py','.yml','.yaml','.md'} and 'docs/archive' not in str(p) and 'board-pointer' in p.read_text(errors='ignore')])\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "10 files still read docs/claude/board-pointer.json, which the reset archived. 8 are board machinery that should retire with it; 2 are the live diag relays (comments only). This is the same orphan-reader class as E9 and is its concrete worklist."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "check_pr_landing.py's R13 failure text names the per-branch route (scripts/ops/claim_merge_slot.py --branch-claim) BEFORE, or instead of, docs/claude/session-board.json -- verified by running the rerun command above and reading the emitted text, not by reading the diff.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0002", "loud": true, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- PR #12678", "rerun": "git checkout -B tmp/pi-0002-probe origin/main && printf '{\"tier\":1,\"landing\":\"self\",\"why\":\"probe\"}' > .github/pr-landing/tmp-pi-0002-probe.json && touch .github/pr-automerge-requests/tmp-pi-0002-probe.txt && git add -A && git commit -qm probe && python3 scripts/ci/check_pr_landing.py --base origin/main; # FINDING STILL APPLIES if the failure text names docs/claude/session-board.json before scripts/ops/claim_merge_slot.py"}, "routed_to": "E9 (session_01JVPxS3XjMyiN3J3HpQPkNq) -- folded into the reader sweep, which already owns this exact class", "state": "routed", "what": "pr-landing-guard R13's first remedy message points at docs/claude/session-board.json and docs/claude/board-pointer.json, BOTH ARCHIVED by the 2026-09-21 reset. The route is not broken -- scripts/ops/claim_merge_slot.py --branch-claim writes a per-branch slot and the guard then passes -- but the guard NAMES THE DEAD REGISTER FIRST, so a session following the error text is sent to a file that no longer exists and only reaches the working route by reading the guard's source. Measured 2026-09-21 on branch claude/metis-insights-manager-9qmm63: R13 emitted 'docs/claude/session-board.json does not exist' plus a 'could_not_check' on board-pointer.json, and the per-branch claim then produced 'OK -- state=declared_self_land'. [!] THIS IS THE FOURTH INSTANCE OF THE E9 READER-ORPHAN PATTERN AND AGAIN IT WAS FOUND BY SOMETHING FAILING LOUDLY, NOT BY THE SWEEP. It bites EVERY Tier-1 self-landing PR from here on, so the population is 'all future lanes', not one branch. [!] DO NOT fix it by weakening R13 -- it fails closed on purpose and the loss it guards is real. Fix the REMEDY TEXT and the ordering so the per-branch route is named first, and decide explicitly whether the shared-board fallback should be removed now that its register is archived."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Either the trainer VM has a serialization mechanism that actually exists, or those comments say plainly that heavy work is unserialized and what the consequence is. Reading the comments is not enough -- check whether two concurrent trainer jobs can in fact overlap today.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0001", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP, found via scope-overlap-audit's self-test failing on PR #12674 for a missing board-pointer.json", "rerun": "grep -rln 'board-pointer\\|board_pointer' --include=*.yml --include=*.py . | grep -v '^./docs/archive'"}, "routed_to": "session_01W7iGwkrozCQ8LYRSM8WgGE (build lane, sonnet-5, ceiling $20) -- dispatched 2026-09-21 by the manager on the SAME DAY it appeared in section 0, which is the required disposition. The lane was given the clears_when as its acceptance criterion VERBATIM, including its refusal of a docs-only fix: step one is measuring whether two heavy trainer jobs can in fact overlap today, not reading the comments that claim they cannot. Both outcomes the clears_when allows are open to it -- a mechanism that exists, or every surface dropping the promise and stating the consequence -- but only after the measurement, and it must say which.", "state": "routed", "what": "The trainer VM's cross-session serialization protocol is gone: trainer-diag-relay.yml and trainer-vm-diag.yml both document heavy VM work as serialized by a VM-LANE CLAIM/RELEASE FIFO on the coordination board, which the 2026-09-21 reset archived. Comments only -- the relays still RUN -- but on a 1-OCPU box the serialization they promise no longer exists and nothing says so."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Either check_manager_scope.py identifies the manager from a surface that EXISTS under the post-reset operating model and grades a real manager commit as such -- verified by running it on a branch with known manager commits and seeing a non-zero graded count, not by reading the diff -- or the guard is retired AND the claim that this rule is CI-enforced is removed from BOTH .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md and CLAUDE.md in the same change. A guard that reports green because it could not look is NOT a pass and does not clear this.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0003", "loud": true, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- PR #12678; fifth instance of the E9 reader-orphan pattern and the fifth found by running something rather than by the sweep", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ci/check_manager_scope.py; # FINDING STILL APPLIES while the output reads `no_manager_commits` on a branch whose commits carry a manager session's Claude-Session trailer, or while the lease-holder roster is derived from an archived file"}, "routed_to": "E9 (session_01JVPxS3XjMyiN3J3HpQPkNq) -- same reader-orphan class, same sweep; E9 is already scoped to every consumer of an archived path anywhere", "state": "routed", "what": "THE GUARD THAT ENFORCES 'THE MANAGER MAY NOT TAKE AN ITEM' IS NOW STRUCTURALLY BLIND, AND IT FAILS OPEN. scripts/ci/check_manager_scope.py derives WHO THE MANAGER IS from the git history of docs/claude/work/MANAGER-LEASE.json -- deliberately, and its docstring argues at length why branch-name and register-authorship were both rejected as identifiers. The 2026-09-21 reset ARCHIVED that lease file. Measured 2026-09-21 on branch claude/metis-insights-manager-9qmm63 with four manager commits in the range: the guard returned 'manager-scope: no_manager_commits ... 0 manager commit(s) in 4 commit(s) graded', exit 0. [!] THE EXIT CODE IS THE PROBLEM. A guard that cannot identify its subject reports GREEN, so 'no violation' and 'could not look' are collapsed into one value -- the exact class scripts/ci/check_collapsed_states.py exists to prevent, in a guard whose own docstring says an unrecognised value 'must not arm anything'. [!] .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md and CLAUDE.md BOTH still state this rule is CI-enforced by this script. That claim is now false, and a false claim of enforcement is worse than a stated absence: this repo's record is that the prose rule alone was read and disobeyed THREE TIMES, which is why the mechanism was built. [!] The lease is NOT to be resurrected -- the reset retired it deliberately. So the real question is what identifies the manager under the new operating model, where the manager's register is MANAGER-CHECKLIST.json and lanes are spawned as sessions with recorded ids. The `lane` field on a checklist row is a candidate identifier that did not exist when this guard was written."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A dispatch of at least three fresh lanes completes its first turn with ZERO lanes asking whether the task is legitimate -- measured from list_sessions post_turn_summary, not from the manager's impression. A single lane proves nothing: the failure was 3 of 6, so the denominator must be stated. Partial credit is not a clear.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0004", "loud": true, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- first six-lane dispatch under the post-reset operating model", "rerun": "# Spawn one lane via create_session with a build-task prompt and NO append_system_prompt, then read its status_bucket and post_turn_summary from list_sessions after its first turn. FINDING STILL APPLIES if status_category is 'need_input' with a question about the task's legitimacy rather than about the task itself."}, "state": "queued", "what": "SPAWNED LANES STALL ON AN AUTHENTICITY CHECK, AND THE MANAGER CANNOT ANSWER THEM. Measured 2026-09-21 on the first six-lane dispatch under the new operating model: THREE of six lanes (A3a session_01AR9SV7kwUg8YBq38UVuVSN, A1 session_012LibLs7RYy22hCFWqohV2P, and the PI-20260921-0001 lane session_01W7iGwkrozCQ8LYRSM8WgGE) ended their FIRST turn without doing any work, each asking whether its own task prompt was legitimate or injected. The cause is structural, not a model quirk: a session created via create_session receives the manager's dispatch with NO human turn in the conversation, and a task that says 'edit infra and open a PR' arriving with no visible human sender is exactly what a session is supposed to be suspicious of. The lanes were RIGHT to ask. [!] THE MANAGER HAS NO DIRECT REPLY CHANNEL. There is no send_message tool in the manager's toolset and ListAgents does not list cloud sessions, so the only working path found was create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) followed by fire_trigger -- which DID deliver and unblocked two of the three. The third was refused by the auto-mode permission classifier, correctly, because that lane was sitting on a pending AskUserQuestion and firing into it would have answered a permission prompt on the operator's behalf. So one lane stays blocked until a human clicks. [!] THE COST IS NOT THE FIX-UP, IT IS THE SILENCE: a stalled lane reads as `idle` in list_sessions, which is what a FINISHED lane also reads as. A manager that did not inspect status_bucket and post_turn_summary would have recorded six lanes dispatched and come back to three that never started. [!] THE LIKELY FIX is create_session's `append_system_prompt`, which puts provenance where a lane reads it before its first tool call, plus naming the in-repo artifacts that CORROBORATE the dispatch (the checklist row, the pipeline item whose routed_to names the lane's own session id) so the lane can verify rather than trust. Confirming a suspicious instruction by asserting harder is not the answer; pointing at independently checkable evidence is."}
