 for a lane to write under .claude/skills/. [!] UNTIL (b) LANDS THIS FIX DIES WITH THIS SESSION, which is precisely the class of loss the operator named as the number-one failure mode. That is the decision being put to them, and it is one line either way.", "state": "routed", "what": "SPAWNED LANES STALL ON AN AUTHENTICITY CHECK, AND THE MANAGER CANNOT ANSWER THEM. Measured 2026-09-21 on the first six-lane dispatch under the new operating model: THREE of six lanes (A3a session_01AR9SV7kwUg8YBq38UVuVSN, A1 session_012LibLs7RYy22hCFWqohV2P, and the PI-20260921-0001 lane session_01W7iGwkrozCQ8LYRSM8WgGE) ended their FIRST turn without doing any work, each asking whether its own task prompt was legitimate or injected. The cause is structural, not a model quirk: a session created via create_session receives the manager's dispatch with NO human turn in the conversation, and a task that says 'edit infra and open a PR' arriving with no visible human sender is exactly what a session is supposed to be suspicious of. The lanes were RIGHT to ask. [!] THE MANAGER HAS NO DIRECT REPLY CHANNEL. There is no send_message tool in the manager's toolset and ListAgents does not list cloud sessions, so the only working path found was create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) followed by fire_trigger -- which DID deliver and unblocked two of the three. The third was refused by the auto-mode permission classifier, correctly, because that lane was sitting on a pending AskUserQuestion and firing into it would have answered a permission prompt on the operator's behalf. So one lane stays blocked until a human clicks. [!] THE COST IS NOT THE FIX-UP, IT IS THE SILENCE: a stalled lane reads as `idle` in list_sessions, which is what a FINISHED lane also reads as. A manager that did not inspect status_bucket and post_turn_summary would have recorded six lanes dispatched and come back to three that never started. [!] THE LIKELY FIX is create_session's `append_system_prompt`, which puts provenance where a lane reads it before its first tool call, plus naming the in-repo artifacts that CORROBORATE the dispatch (the checklist row, the pipeline item whose routed_to names the lane's own session id) so the lane can verify rather than trust. Confirming a suspicious instruction by asserting harder is not the answer; pointing at independently checkable evidence is."}
