{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A dispatch of at least three fresh lanes completes its first turn with ZERO lanes asking whether the task is legitimate -- measured from list_sessions post_turn_summary, not from the manager's impression. A single lane proves nothing: the failure was 3 of 6, so the denominator must be stated. Partial credit is not a clear.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M03", "loud": true, "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- first six-lane dispatch under the post-reset operating model", "rerun": "# Spawn one lane via create_session with a build-task prompt and NO append_system_prompt, then read its status_bucket and post_turn_summary from list_sessions after its first turn. FINDING STILL APPLIES if status_category is 'need_input' with a question about the task's legitimacy rather than about the task itself."}, "routed_to": "THE OPERATOR, via section 2 of the next daily brief -- because the durable half of this fix CANNOT be delegated. [!] MEASURED 2026-09-21: the manager attempted to spawn a lane to write the spawn recipe into .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md and the create_session call was REFUSED by the auto-mode permission classifier, reason `[Self-Modification]` -- an agent spawning another agent to rewrite its own operating instructions. That refusal is CORRECT and was not worked around. [!] SO THE ITEM SPLITS, and the halves have different owners. (a) THE BEHAVIOURAL HALF IS ALREADY APPLIED and needs nobody: the manager now passes append_system_prompt carrying provenance plus the in-repo artifacts that let a lane VERIFY rather than trust, and now reads status_bucket + post_turn_summary after every dispatch rather than treating `idle` as finished. (b) THE DURABLE HALF -- writing that recipe into the manager contract so the NEXT manager inherits it -- needs either the operator to make the edit, or an explicit operator grant for a lane to write under .claude/skills/. [!] UNTIL (b) LANDS THIS FIX DIES WITH THIS SESSION, which is precisely the class of loss the operator named as the number-one failure mode. That is the decision being put to them, and it is one line either way.", "state": "routed", "what": "SPAWNED LANES STALL ON AN AUTHENTICITY CHECK, AND THE MANAGER CANNOT ANSWER THEM. Measured 2026-09-21 on the first six-lane dispatch under the new operating model: THREE of six lanes (A3a session_01AR9SV7kwUg8YBq38UVuVSN, A1 session_012LibLs7RYy22hCFWqohV2P, and the PI-20260921-0001 lane session_01W7iGwkrozCQ8LYRSM8WgGE) ended their FIRST turn without doing any work, each asking whether its own task prompt was legitimate or injected. The cause is structural, not a model quirk: a session created via create_session receives the manager's dispatch with NO human turn in the conversation, and a task that says 'edit infra and open a PR' arriving with no visible human sender is exactly what a session is supposed to be suspicious of. The lanes were RIGHT to ask. [!] THE MANAGER HAS NO DIRECT REPLY CHANNEL. There is no send_message tool in the manager's toolset and ListAgents does not list cloud sessions, so the only working path found was create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) followed by fire_trigger -- which DID deliver and unblocked two of the three. The third was refused by the auto-mode permission classifier, correctly, because that lane was sitting on a pending AskUserQuestion and firing into it would have answered a permission prompt on the operator's behalf. So one lane stays blocked until a human clicks. [!] THE COST IS NOT THE FIX-UP, IT IS THE SILENCE: a stalled lane reads as `idle` in list_sessions, which is what a FINISHED lane also reads as. A manager that did not inspect status_bucket and post_turn_summary would have recorded six lanes dispatched and come back to three that never started. [!] THE LIKELY FIX is create_session's `append_system_prompt`, which puts provenance where a lane reads it before its first tool call, plus naming the in-repo artifacts that CORROBORATE the dispatch (the checklist row, the pipeline item whose routed_to names the lane's own session id) so the lane can verify rather than trust. Confirming a suspicious instruction by asserting harder is not the answer; pointing at independently checkable evidence is. [!] REPLY-CHANNEL MECHANICS, MEASURED 2026-09-21 so the next manager does not re-derive them at a lane's expense. create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) then fire_trigger(trigger_id) with NO other argument delivers into that lane (confirmed: the result's session_id came back as cse_<same suffix as the lane>). Passing fire_trigger's optional `text` argument does NOT do that -- it SPAWNED A FRESH SESSION instead (session_016iHXA8iHzCaJkcmxU32ALR, titled '# unblock A1', origin force_run_trigger, no repo sources, $0.18 burned doing nothing; archived). So: fire bare. [!] AND THE SAME CALL IS REFUSED, CORRECTLY, when the target lane holds a pending permission prompt -- firing into it would answer that prompt on the operator's behalf. That refusal is a boundary to respect, not a bug to route around; such a lane stays blocked until a human clicks, and the manager's job is to SURFACE it, not to clear it. [!] ID REASSIGNED 2026-09-21 BY THE MANAGER THAT CAUSED THE COLLISION. This finding was first appended under PI-20260921-0002/0003/0004, ids that another session had already minted or was concurrently minting for DIFFERENT findings. Because load() folds to LAST-RECORD-WINS, mine displaced theirs silently and `--check` still reported `clean`. The record is re-filed here under a manager-scoped id; the original records stay in the log, which is the audit trail working as designed. See PI-20260921-M04 for the tool defect that allowed it."}
