{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "pipeline.append() refuses, or at minimum loudly flags, an append whose id already exists with materially different content -- verified by ACTUALLY ATTEMPTING one and seeing it refused, not by reading the diff -- and pipeline --check reports displaced/shadowed ids rather than `clean`. A fix that only renames ids without adding the check does NOT clear this.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M04", "loud": true, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- found while resolving the E9 merge; caused by this same session", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys;sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/ops');import pipeline;r=pipeline.read_log();print('records',r.records,'items',len(r.items))\"; # FINDING STILL APPLIES while records>items for reasons other than genuine state changes, and while append() accepts an existing id carrying unrelated content without complaint"}, "state": "queued", "what": "pipeline.append() HAS NO ID COLLISION CHECK, SO ONE FINDING CAN SILENTLY DISPLACE ANOTHER AND THE STORE STILL READS `clean`. MEASURED 2026-09-21, and it is not hypothetical -- the manager session did it. The store's design is APPEND-ONLY with LAST-RECORD-WINS, which is correct for a STATE CHANGE to a known item and is what gives the audit trail for free. But nothing distinguishes 'a new state for item X' from 'a DIFFERENT finding that happens to reuse X's id'. Two sessions filing concurrently both mint the next free number from the store they each read at start, and neither can see the other. Observed: main carried PI-20260921-0002 (a board-pointer reader finding, filed by session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP in #12676); the manager appended a pr-landing-guard finding under the same id; load() folded 8 records to 4 items and the FIRST finding vanished from items, due() and render_section_0 entirely. Concurrently the E9 lane minted its OWN 0002/0003/0004/0005 on its branch for four further different findings, so the same collision was queued up three more times behind a merge. [!] `--check` REPORTED `clean -- 4 item(s) validated over 8 record(s), 0 unreadable` THROUGHOUT. That is the serious half: the store cannot distinguish 'this item is gone' from 'this item was never filed', which is the exact collapsed-state class scripts/ci/check_collapsed_states.py enforces against repo-wide, occurring inside the module built to stop findings being dropped. A displaced row is a DROPPED FINDING that looks like a healthy store. [!] DO NOT fix it by making ids opaque (a uuid) and stopping there -- the readable dated id is worth keeping and the real defect is the missing check. append() should refuse an id already present whose record is not plausibly a state change of it, and the caller should have to say which it means. Allocation needs an answer too: two sessions that cannot see each other will keep picking the same next number."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "pipeline.append() refuses, or at minimum loudly flags, an append whose id already exists with materially different content -- verified by ACTUALLY ATTEMPTING one and seeing it refused, not by reading the diff -- and pipeline --check reports displaced/shadowed ids rather than `clean`. A fix that only renames ids without adding the check does NOT clear this.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M04", "loud": true, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- found while resolving the E9 merge; caused by this same session", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys;sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/ops');import pipeline;r=pipeline.read_log();print('records',r.records,'items',len(r.items))\"; # FINDING STILL APPLIES while records>items for reasons other than genuine state changes, and while append() accepts an existing id carrying unrelated content without complaint"}, "routed_to": "session_01YUnvQssdGXkpjB2MRkddF3 (build lane, sonnet-5, ceiling $18) — dispatched 2026-09-21, the same day this appeared unrouted in section 0. Fenced OUT of render_section_0 and unrouted_count signatures (A3a now CALLS them) and out of PIPELINE.jsonl beyond its own state change (A8 is importing ~1,150 rows into it), and told the store will be that size so the fold cost of any collision check is a real constraint rather than a detail.", "state": "routed", "what": "pipeline.append() HAS NO ID COLLISION CHECK, SO ONE FINDING CAN SILENTLY DISPLACE ANOTHER AND THE STORE STILL READS `clean`. MEASURED 2026-09-21, and it is not hypothetical -- the manager session did it. The store's design is APPEND-ONLY with LAST-RECORD-WINS, which is correct for a STATE CHANGE to a known item and is what gives the audit trail for free. But nothing distinguishes 'a new state for item X' from 'a DIFFERENT finding that happens to reuse X's id'. Two sessions filing concurrently both mint the next free number from the store they each read at start, and neither can see the other. Observed: main carried PI-20260921-0002 (a board-pointer reader finding, filed by session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP in #12676); the manager appended a pr-landing-guard finding under the same id; load() folded 8 records to 4 items and the FIRST finding vanished from items, due() and render_section_0 entirely. Concurrently the E9 lane minted its OWN 0002/0003/0004/0005 on its branch for four further different findings, so the same collision was queued up three more times behind a merge. [!] `--check` REPORTED `clean -- 4 item(s) validated over 8 record(s), 0 unreadable` THROUGHOUT. That is the serious half: the store cannot distinguish 'this item is gone' from 'this item was never filed', which is the exact collapsed-state class scripts/ci/check_collapsed_states.py enforces against repo-wide, occurring inside the module built to stop findings being dropped. A displaced row is a DROPPED FINDING that looks like a healthy store. [!] DO NOT fix it by making ids opaque (a uuid) and stopping there -- the readable dated id is worth keeping and the real defect is the missing check. append() should refuse an id already present whose record is not plausibly a state change of it, and the caller should have to say which it means. Allocation needs an answer too: two sessions that cannot see each other will keep picking the same next number."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Every one of the 10 either no longer references the archived path, or carries a dated note saying why it stays. The COUNT going to zero is the check.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0002", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "E9 / PR #12674", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import pathlib;print([p for p in pathlib.Path('.').rglob('*') if p.suffix in {'.py','.yml','.yaml','.md'} and 'docs/archive' not in str(p) and 'board-pointer' in p.read_text(errors='ignore')])\""}, "routed_to": "E9 (session_01JVPxS3XjMyiN3J3HpQPkNq) — this finding is the board-pointer half of the reader sweep E9 already owns, and E9 independently re-derived it AND corrected its rerun command on its own branch. Routing it here rather than re-dispatching: the work is in E9's PR, not pending. ⚠️ Routed only AFTER being restored — it had been silently displaced by an id collision (PI-20260921-M04) and was invisible to due() and section 0 while it sat on main.", "state": "routed", "what": "10 files still read docs/claude/board-pointer.json, which the reset archived. 8 are board machinery that should retire with it; 2 are the live diag relays (comments only). This is the same orphan-reader class as E9 and is its concrete worklist. [!] RE-APPENDED 2026-09-21 TO RESTORE THIS ID TO ITS ORIGINAL FINDING. A later manager session appended a DIFFERENT finding under this same id, which silently became the current state of PI-20260921-0002 and removed this one from due() and section 0 entirely, with `--check` still reporting `clean`. Nothing about this finding changed; only its visibility was restored. The intruding finding now lives at PI-20260921-M01."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A dispatch of at least three fresh lanes completes its first turn with ZERO lanes asking whether the task is legitimate -- measured from list_sessions post_turn_summary, not from the manager's impression. A single lane proves nothing: the failure was 3 of 6, so the denominator must be stated. Partial credit is not a clear.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0004", "loud": true, "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- first six-lane dispatch under the post-reset operating model", "rerun": "# Spawn one lane via create_session with a build-task prompt and NO append_system_prompt, then read its status_bucket and post_turn_summary from list_sessions after its first turn. FINDING STILL APPLIES if status_category is 'need_input' with a question about the task's legitimacy rather than about the task itself."}, "routed_to": "THE OPERATOR, via section 2 of the next daily brief -- because the durable half of this fix CANNOT be delegated. [!] MEASURED 2026-09-21: the manager attempted to spawn a lane to write the spawn recipe into .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md and the create_session call was REFUSED by the auto-mode permission classifier, reason `[Self-Modification]` -- an agent spawning another agent to rewrite its own operating instructions. That refusal is CORRECT and was not worked around. [!] SO THE ITEM SPLITS, and the halves have different owners. (a) THE BEHAVIOURAL HALF IS ALREADY APPLIED and needs nobody: the manager now passes append_system_prompt carrying provenance plus the in-repo artifacts that let a lane VERIFY rather than trust, and now reads status_bucket + post_turn_summary after every dispatch rather than treating `idle` as finished. (b) THE DURABLE HALF -- writing that recipe into the manager contract so the NEXT manager inherits it -- needs either the operator to make the edit, or an explicit operator grant for a lane to write under .claude/skills/. [!] UNTIL (b) LANDS THIS FIX DIES WITH THIS SESSION, which is precisely the class of loss the operator named as the number-one failure mode. That is the decision being put to them, and it is one line either way.", "state": "done", "terminal_reason": "BOTH HALVES LANDED 2026-09-21. The behavioural half was applied in-session (append_system_prompt carrying provenance plus the in-repo artifacts a lane can verify against; status_bucket + post_turn_summary read after every dispatch instead of session_status). The DURABLE half — the half that was the actual decision, because without it the fix died with the session — is now written into .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md § \"Every spawn carries provenance, and every dispatch is verified\". [!] IT NEEDED AN OPERATOR GRANT AND GOT ONE, verbatim 2026-09-21: \"You have permission to make this fix permanent.\" A lane spawned to make the edit had been refused [Self-Modification], and that refusal was never worked around; the edit is recorded under the guard's own argued escape hatch at docs/claude/work/manager-scope-exception.yaml (MGR-20260921-SPAWN-PROVENANCE, expires 2026-09-24, naming that one path and nothing else). [!] NOT YET OBSERVED, and this is the honest limit of the claim: the recipe is WRITTEN, not PROVEN. Its own clears_when asked for a dispatch of at least three fresh lanes completing their first turn with ZERO legitimacy questions, denominator stated. The two lanes spawned since carrying the provenance block (E3, E10, PI-M04) did start work without asking — 3 of 3 — but they were spawned by the session that wrote the recipe, not by a successor reading it, which is the case that matters. A successor manager should re-measure and say so.", "what": "SPAWNED LANES STALL ON AN AUTHENTICITY CHECK, AND THE MANAGER CANNOT ANSWER THEM. Measured 2026-09-21 on the first six-lane dispatch under the new operating model: THREE of six lanes (A3a session_01AR9SV7kwUg8YBq38UVuVSN, A1 session_012LibLs7RYy22hCFWqohV2P, and the PI-20260921-0001 lane session_01W7iGwkrozCQ8LYRSM8WgGE) ended their FIRST turn without doing any work, each asking whether its own task prompt was legitimate or injected. The cause is structural, not a model quirk: a session created via create_session receives the manager's dispatch with NO human turn in the conversation, and a task that says 'edit infra and open a PR' arriving with no visible human sender is exactly what a session is supposed to be suspicious of. The lanes were RIGHT to ask. [!] THE MANAGER HAS NO DIRECT REPLY CHANNEL. There is no send_message tool in the manager's toolset and ListAgents does not list cloud sessions, so the only working path found was create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) followed by fire_trigger -- which DID deliver and unblocked two of the three. The third was refused by the auto-mode permission classifier, correctly, because that lane was sitting on a pending AskUserQuestion and firing into it would have answered a permission prompt on the operator's behalf. So one lane stays blocked until a human clicks. [!] THE COST IS NOT THE FIX-UP, IT IS THE SILENCE: a stalled lane reads as `idle` in list_sessions, which is what a FINISHED lane also reads as. A manager that did not inspect status_bucket and post_turn_summary would have recorded six lanes dispatched and come back to three that never started. [!] THE LIKELY FIX is create_session's `append_system_prompt`, which puts provenance where a lane reads it before its first tool call, plus naming the in-repo artifacts that CORROBORATE the dispatch (the checklist row, the pipeline item whose routed_to names the lane's own session id) so the lane can verify rather than trust. Confirming a suspicious instruction by asserting harder is not the answer; pointing at independently checkable evidence is. [!] REPLY-CHANNEL MECHANICS, MEASURED 2026-09-21 so the next manager does not re-derive them at a lane's expense. create_trigger(persistent_session_id=<lane>) then fire_trigger(trigger_id) with NO other argument delivers into that lane (confirmed: the result's session_id came back as cse_<same suffix as the lane>). Passing fire_trigger's optional `text` argument does NOT do that -- it SPAWNED A FRESH SESSION instead (session_016iHXA8iHzCaJkcmxU32ALR, titled '# unblock A1', origin force_run_trigger, no repo sources, $0.18 burned doing nothing; archived). So: fire bare. [!] AND THE SAME CALL IS REFUSED, CORRECTLY, when the target lane holds a pending permission prompt -- firing into it would answer that prompt on the operator's behalf. That refusal is a boundary to respect, not a bug to route around; such a lane stays blocked until a human clicks, and the manager's job is to SURFACE it, not to clear it."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "The command in origin.rerun prints NOTHING against .claude/settings.json -- i.e. the hooks name no archived register and no retired skill. Verified by RUNNING it, not by reading a diff. A partial cleanup that leaves any of the 22 does not clear this, because a session cannot tell which of the instructions it was just given are the live ones.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M05", "loud": true, "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/audits/archived-register-readers-fable-probe.md (E11 Fable probe, merged #12681); re-counted independently by session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH before filing", "rerun": "grep -oE '(board-pointer|session-board|OPEN-ITEMS|health-review-backlog|performance-review-backlog|ml-review-backlog|research-review-backlog|system-review|delegate-work|MANAGER-LEASE|SESSIONS\\.json|duty)' .claude/settings.json | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn; # FINDING STILL APPLIES while this prints any line. Expected clear state: no output."}, "routed_to": "THE OPERATOR, via section 2 of the next daily brief. Not delegable: .claude/ is harness configuration, an agent editing it is refused [Self-Modification], and that refusal is correct and was not worked around. What is needed is either the operator making the edit or an explicit grant naming .claude/settings.json, the way the spawn-provenance grant named manager/SKILL.md.", "state": "routed", "what": "EVERY SESSION IN THIS REPO IS TOLD, BEFORE ITS FIRST TOOL CALL, TO USE FILES THE RESET ARCHIVED. .claude/settings.json's SessionStart, PreToolUse and Stop hooks carry 22 references to archived paths and retired skills -- COUNTED 2026-09-21 by the manager, independently of the report that raised it: session-board x6, board-pointer x6, /system-review x4, health-review-backlog x2, performance-review-backlog, ml-review-backlog, `duty`, `delegate-work`. [!] THIS IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE INSTANCE OF THE ORPHAN CLASS FOUND SO FAR, because it is not a degraded check a session might never run -- it is the FIRST THING EVERY SESSION READS, and it instructs them to register on a coordination board that does not exist and to file findings into backlogs nothing reads. It is a live tax on every lane this manager spawns. [!] IT IS ALSO PLAUSIBLY A CONTRIBUTING CAUSE OF PI-20260921-0004: three of six lanes opened by questioning whether their task was injected, and one named the hook text specifically as part of what made the session look wrong -- a startup block issuing confident instructions about absent files is exactly the shape of a prompt-injection tell. That link is PLAUSIBLE AND NOT ESTABLISHED; the stall reproduced for other reasons too and the hooks were never isolated as a variable. [!] THE OPERATOR MUST DO THIS ONE OR EXPLICITLY GRANT IT. .claude/settings.json is harness configuration and an agent editing it is refused [Self-Modification] -- the manager confirmed that refusal twice today on the adjacent .claude/skills path. The operator's 2026-09-21 grant covered the spawn-provenance fix specifically and is NOT being stretched to cover this: an approval in one context does not extend to the next."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Either a mechanism exists that ties a checklist row's state to the PR that resolved it -- demonstrated by making a merged PR name a still-`queued` id and seeing something fail -- or the manager contract states plainly that the register's state is hand-maintained and MAY be stale in both directions, so a reader checks git before dispatching. Verified by running the demonstration, not by reading a diff. A one-off correction of E10 does NOT clear this; E10 is already corrected and the mechanism is what is missing.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M06", "loud": false, "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- found by dispatching a lane against a stale row", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json,subprocess;d=json.load(open('docs/claude/work/MANAGER-CHECKLIST.json'));q=[i['id'] for i in d['items'] if i['state']=='queued' and not i.get('prs')];print('queued rows carrying no PR:',q)\"; git log --oneline -40 main | grep -iE 'E1[0-9]|A[1-9]|B[1-9]'; # FINDING STILL APPLIES if any id in the first list appears in the second as already landed"}, "state": "queued", "what": "A CHECKLIST ROW WAS NOT MOVED OFF `queued` BY THE PR THAT RESOLVED IT, AND THE REGISTER THEREFORE LIED TOWARD MORE WORK. MEASURED 2026-09-21: row E10 read `queued` while its fix was already merged in #12676 -- the same PR the session handoff described as 'A7's pipeline + close-out contract + E10'. The manager read the ROW, dispatched a lane against it, and the lane correctly reported there was nothing to do. Cost $0.93, which is small; the mechanism is not. [!] THIS IS THE MIRROR OF THE FAILURE THE WHOLE RESET IS ABOUT. The reset's concern is work that silently stops; this is work that silently RESTARTS, and both come from the same root -- the register's state is maintained by hand, by whoever remembers, and nothing ties a row's state to the PR that changed it. A row stuck at `queued` past its fix costs a duplicate dispatch; the same gap in the other direction is a row marked `done` that nobody did. [!] THE CHECKLIST HAS NO `prs` DISCIPLINE EITHER: E10's `prs` array was empty although #12676 carried its fix, so even a reader who doubted the state had nothing to follow. [!] CANDIDATE FIX, not prescribed: a guard that fails when a merged PR's body or commits name a checklist id whose row is still `queued`. That is checkable from git alone and needs no new register."}
